    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (the `POST /api/owners` handler), which is where all the existing business-rule rejections live.\n\n**Change** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\n1. Added a check in `addOwner`, right after the `registrationDate` is defaulted to today and before the save, that rejects with **HTTP 429** when the daily cap is reached:\n   ```java\n   if (countOwnersRegisteredToday() >= DAILY_CREATE_LIMIT) {\n       throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS,\n           \"the maximum number of owners that may be created today has been reached\");\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. Added a `DAILY_CREATE_LIMIT = 100` constant and a `countOwnersRegisteredToday()` helper that counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()`, mirroring the existing in-memory counting pattern (`countOwnersInCity`, `countNamesakes`).\n\nThis follows the codebase's established conventions: `ResponseStatusException` is already mapped to its verbatim status by the global `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, so `TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` surfaces as a real 429 \u2014 no changes needed elsewhere. The 101st create is rejected once 100 owners already carry today's date (the `>=` threshold matches the existing `>= CITY_CAPACITY` semantics), while seed owners have a null `registrationDate` and so don't count.\n\nThe pre-existing acceptance test `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (create 100, expect the 101st to return 429) now passes, and the full suite is green.",
